Hence, ROI is not the say all be all measure of the value of an investment.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.040
Okay, now let's look at this time value of money.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.040
Continuous compounding.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.360
So, let's assume you have a savings account and it generates 10% interest per year over

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:18.360
20 years.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.720
Initially, you invest $1 into this account.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:26.200
Over the course of 20 years, with that 10% interest rate, the value of your account will

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.400
rise to $6.73.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.080
That's 6.73 times the amount you initially invested.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:38.120
And this is the power of continuous compounding or compound interest.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.880
So, time value of money.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.480
This concept says that money is worth more today than in the future because of the potential

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:50.240
earning capacity that money has, as seen in the example, and because the power of money

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.080
decreases due to macroeconomic effects such as inflation.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.560
So, would you prefer $1 today or $1 in 5 years time?

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:03.180
With our previous example, we can deduce that we would like $1 today.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:07.720
This is because we have the potential cash flows that money can create during this period

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:14.140
as well as the avoiding of the potential effects of inflation.

