Now we have net present value, the last of our three key concepts for this section of

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:19.880
the video.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:23.640
So, net present value is a pretty straightforward concept.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.280
And it's as the name suggests, the net of all cash inflows and cash outflows to determine

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.480
the value of an asset.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:36.760
Cash inflows and cash outflows essentially represent the income and the sunken costs

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.600
that an investment has taken.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.640
So, how do we calculate the net present value?

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.600
But before we can calculate it, we need to understand, net present value is an important

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.840
tool to determine the value of an investment.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.360
If net present value is greater than zero, that means an investment was a positive investment

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.840
generating good returns.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.680
Before we can calculate what net present value is, we have to understand something called

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.400
the discount rate.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:05.800
Discount rate is the interest rate used to determine the present value of future cash

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.920
flows in a discounted cash flow analysis.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.000
Essentially it's the interest rate that the Federal Reserve charges on short term loans.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.900
We use it to calculate the value of money in the future due to macroeconomic policies

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.280
like inflation which decrease the value of future cash flows.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.080
So, once we understand what the discount rate is, we can accurately calculate the net present

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.080
value.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.800
So, let's assume you purchased a vending machine.

