00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.160
So first of all, what is a stock?

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:39.600
So a stock is a security that represents a portion of ownership in a company.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.520
So people can only purchase stocks from a public or private type of company.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.840
Public companies are companies that trade their stock on a public platform, or anybody

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.560
can purchase it.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.980
Examples are Amazon, Apple, and Tesla.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:58.380
If you wanted to right now, you could go and purchase their stock.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:02.000
Private companies, on the other hand, are held by certain owners, typically the people

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.680
who started the company.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.040
In certain situations, they may choose to sell their stock, but not on a public platform,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.320
only privately to maybe their friends and family.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.120
So what are stocks issued?

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:17.220
Stocks are issued by companies to raise finances for their goals.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:21.900
These may be expansion, raising money for inventory, and so on.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.760
Stocks are also referred to as equity.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:30.200
And as many of you have seen in the Shark Tank show, we see the term equity thrown around.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:36.320
In this example, Kevin offers $400,000 for an $8.2 million valuation.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.760
So the company's total equity, or stock, is worth $8.2 million.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:47.000
And Kevin's offering $400,000, which represents 4.8% of that total equity, so he would be

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.520
owning 4.85% of the total stock of that company.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.400
So now what is a bond?

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:58.000
A bond is an instrument that represents a loan made by an investor, which is you and

00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:59.340
a borrower.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:04.580
So bonds can be issued by a variety of places, unlike stocks which are only issued by firms.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:11.520
Bonds can be issued by firms, governments, states, and other government-affiliated organizations.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.920
Bonds have four main features.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.560
Their issue price, which is what you buy it for.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.180
The face value, which includes the price you're buying it for, as well as the interest payments.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:25.560
The coupon rate, or the interest rate, which is the set of payments you'll be receiving

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:27.680
over the course of the bond.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:31.480
The coupon date, which is when these payments will be made to you.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:36.920
And the maturity date, which is when a lifetime of the bond is expended and the issue price

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:38.840
is returned to you.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:43.920
So bonds are used typically to fund projects for these organizations.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.000
They only last for a certain period of time, as determined by the maturity rate when the

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:54.880
bond is issued, and they either have fixed or variable payments.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.320
Something interesting about bonds is that they're inversely related to interest rates

00:13:58.320 --> 00:13:59.320
in the market.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:05.000
This is because as interest rates rise, the lower coupon rate of the bonds become less

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.000
desirable.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:11.400
As a result, bond issuers lower their price.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:15.640
An example of a government-funded project could be developing infrastructure, such as

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:19.280
