sell.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.320
But once your bond reaches a maturity, you're almost guaranteed to make profits, unless

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:30.560
the company or organization that you purchase the bond from defaults or goes bankrupt.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.640
So how do we value stocks on any asset in general?

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:39.220
Well there's two main factors that we have to include when trying to value a stock or

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:41.760
bond or any asset for that matter.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:45.640
The expected cash flows and the risk associated with the value.

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:50.680
As we discussed earlier, cash flows are the total revenues we'll be making, along with

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:54.040
the sunken costs of bursting an asset.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.240
And risk.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:17:00.220
Risk can be represented with the chance for financial losses or the chance that a company

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:03.400
even goes bankrupt and you lose your value.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.480
Okay, so stock valuation.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.120
There is a lot of uncertainty when valuing a stock, and part of this is due to the fact

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:13.960
that there's so many different ways to value a stock.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:18.000
For example, there's the discounted cash flow method, which we'll be looking into next.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:22.880
There's the constant growth divided by dividend discount method, and there's also techniques

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:27.640
called comparables, which we'll also be discussing, such as price to earnings, price to books,

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:29.180
and price to sales.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:33.840
As a result, there's no standard convention to measure a stock, and when people are comparing

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:38.000
stocks, it's best to use the most information you have available to you and try and do all

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:42.360
these techniques, which is, of course, unviable in the real world though.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:45.760
So, the discounted cash flow method.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:49.320
Discounted cash flow method is similar to what we discussed in the previous section,

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:54.640
where we take all the expected cash flows for an asset, including the sunken costs and

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:59.280
the revenues that it slightly generate in the future, and discount these values, meaning

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:05.600
we adjust the value of money in accordance with macroeconomic features like inflation

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:10.380
and so on to ensure that the future value of money is converted to the present value

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:11.380
of money.

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:14.680
So, what are the pros of this technique?

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:19.760
Theoretically, this is the most sound method if the analyst is confident in their assumptions

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:20.760
and forecasts.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:26.240
Additionally, it's not significantly influenced by temporary market conditions or non-economic

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:27.480
factors.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:33.400
Like if the industry is suffering from a temporary lack of resources, this technique ensures

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:37.440
that those factors are overcome when calculating the value.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:43.280
And this is especially useful when a company you're valuing is in an industry where there's

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:48.360
no other competitors or very limited competitors, so that way there's limited or no comparable

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:49.560
information.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:54.840
As a result, you only base your forecasts off the individual company.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:59.440
The cons of this technique are that it can vary over a wide range of values, because

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:03.680
the predictions are based on forecasts which are likely to be inaccurate.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:08.080
Additionally, it's very time intensive compared to the other evaluation techniques which we'll

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:09.840
discuss later.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:14.080
And additionally, forecasting future performance is very difficult since it requires lots of

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:20.280
statistical tools, and again, it is likely to be inaccurate or imprecise.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:23.800
So valuation frameworks, we'll be looking at comps now.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.640
So what are comps?

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:31.560
Comps are also called comparables or multiples, and it is a valuation technique that is based

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:35.000
on comparing companies within an industry.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.280
It involves using metrics such as price to earnings, price to books, and other relevant

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:43.160
ratios to that of similar companies in the same industry.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:47.720
The rationale behind using comps lies in the principle that companies within the same industry

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:52.920
tend to have similar financial and operational characteristics due to shared market and economic

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.280
factors.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:58.880
By evaluating the valuation of a company against its peers, investors can gain insights into

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:05.120
its performance within the industry and whether it's over, under, or fairly valued.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:10.620
So the two most popular forms of comps are price to earnings ratio and something called

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:17.960
enterprise value to EDITDA or EBITDA.

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:20.580
So these are examples of certain types of comps.

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:25.240
We have price to earnings, price to sales, enterprise value to earning before interest,

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:29.160
tax, and appreciation, price to cash flow, and price to earnings.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:33.860
The reason these are better than discounting cash flow in terms of time is that their ratios

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:36.440
so they can be calculated very quickly.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:40.460
All you have to do is go to the financial documents of a company, look at their numbers

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:44.960
of their reporting, and calculate them, which allows for swift and easy comparison between

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.120
companies within the same industry.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:52.380
Alright, in this section we'll be going over business strategy and any of the financial

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.200
documents related to it.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.160
So first, what is business strategy?

00:20:56.160 --> 00:21:02.480
Well, it's a plan of action or a policy designed to achieve the major or overall aim of a company.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:06.760
So it's the time to develop a coherent economic strategy and the plans to ensure the future

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:11.600
success of the company in both its goals and financial success.

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:15.820
So one key aspect of the business strategy is the mission statement.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.020
The mission statement is the summary of the aims and the values of a company.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:24.660
The key elements of a mission statement are the purpose, the target audience, an explanation

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:29.520
of the business, how the business is unique, and the values that the business has.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:32.560
And it has to do all of these things in just a couple sentences.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.240
So let's look at a couple examples.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:39.620
Here we have Microsoft, and their business statement is to empower every person and every

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:43.560
organization on the planet to achieve more.

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:48.080
We have Honda, maintaining a global viewpoint, we are dedicated to supplying products of

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:53.200
the highest quality yet at a reasonable price for worldwide customer satisfaction.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:57.960
And Walmart, to save people money so that they can live better.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:01.080
Across these three statements, there's common features.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:06.680
One, they're short, two, they're easy to remember, and three, they showcase the core

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.960
values and missions of these companies.

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:13.800
Okay, so the next part about a company analysis.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:18.440
There's various tools that companies include to create their business statement.

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:21.600
And one of these principle tools is a SWOT analysis.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:26.560
A SWOT analysis helps a company position themselves and understand where they are in the marketplace

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:28.600
against their competitors.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:34.080
There are four key elements of SWOT analysis, strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats,

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.920
which thus produce the name SWOT.

00:22:35.920 --> 00:22:40.600
So the strengths of a company are its competitive advantage, the assets that it had that are

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.400
proprietary and products that are performing well.

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:47.920
These are the strengths because they are unique features of a company that other competitions

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:49.640
don't have.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:53.920
The weaknesses of a company are things that they lack that their competitors have, things

00:22:53.920 --> 00:23:00.280
that their competitors do better and any resource limitations that a company may be experiencing.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:04.920
So strengths and weaknesses are within the company and its operations.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:07.560
Then we have opportunities and threats.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:14.200
Threats are new legislature which threatens a company's operations, any new emerging competition,

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.320
and changes to consumer attitudes and opinions.

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:23.100
Opportunities are technologies that a company can integrate to improve its processes, emerging

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:28.360
press and media which can help a company generate any publicity, and underserved markets that

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:30.960
a company can integrate themselves into.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:34.560
Threats and opportunities are not directly involved in the company and are external and

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.380
can be affecting the company over time.

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:41.340
So here we have a bit more of a detailed explanation of what strengths and weaknesses are.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.320
So strengths are the internal attributes and resources of a company that propel the company

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:46.320
towards success.

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:52.960
It represents the area where your organization outperforms others, evident by both your competitors

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.280
and your customers.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:58.760
Weaknesses are the facets that hinder favorable outcomes for your business.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:02.520
They pinpoint areas that require improvements, shedding light on where your company falls

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:06.840
short compared to its competitors.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:10.120
Opportunities encompass external elements that can be leveraged to your advantage.

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:13.880
These are prevailing market trends that, when capitalized upon using your strengths, can

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:17.160
enhance revenue and provide a competitive edge.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:21.120
Threats denote external factors that jeopardize your organization's success.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.640
These encompass advantages held by competitors.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:27.920
To mitigate these risks, it's crucial to employers' strengths strategically.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:33.320
Swat analysis, when used, can help a company understand where it is now in its industry

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:39.680
and what the future holds and how the company can progress itself in a beneficial way.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:44.680
One more tool that a business can use to help it understand the importance of certain products

00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:48.000
or services it sells is the BCG matrix.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:53.720
The BCG matrix, otherwise known as the Boston Consulting Group matrix, is a business analysis

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:59.160
tool that helps organizations assess their product portfolio's performance and potential.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:05.320
As you can see on the diagram, there are four different categories, stars, cash cows, question

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:10.640
marks and dogs, and you can categorize products depending on their market growth rate and

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.600
their relative market share.

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:19.000
Stars, these are products with high market shares that are in fast-growing markets.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:23.200
They have the potential to generate significant profits due to their position in this industry.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:29.200
So if a company dominates 90% of the market share for a niche that is growing very fast,

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:32.320
the product that they're selling is likely to be a star.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:38.920
Cash cows are products that have high market shares in markets that aren't growing so fast.

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:44.240
While they may not have as much growth potential, they generate steady cash flow, which is often

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:45.840
desirable for businesses.

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:50.440
So similar to stars, but they don't have the future potential and instead they're making

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:52.360
money right now.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:56.400
Question marks are products that companies may have low market share in, however the

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.920
market is likely to expand in the future.

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:03.960
They require consideration as to whether a company should invest in it to develop its

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:09.200
market share and potentially create it into a star or to let it go and become a dog.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:13.560
And dogs are products that have a low market share and a market growth in a market that

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:14.560
isn't growing.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:19.480
They won't generate significant profits for the company and if a company needs to reduce

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:26.640
some of its services that it's offering, the dogs are the first to go.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:31.560
The BCG Matrix can help a company allocate its resources effectively by suggesting strategies

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:36.920
for products or services that fall into each specific quadrant.

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:42.080
And the last strategic business tool we'll talk about is Porter's Generic Strategies.

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:45.720
Porter's Generic Strategies are a set of strategic approaches developed by Michael

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:50.360
Porter, as the name suggests, to help businesses understand where they stand in the industry

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:53.240
and to help them gain a competitive advantage.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:59.040
So again, there are four quadrants in this diagram.

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:04.280
There's cost leadership, differentiation, cost focus, and differentiation focus.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:10.040
So cost leadership is a strategy that focuses on becoming the lowest cost producer in an

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:11.320
industry.

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:16.520
This is achieved by economies of scale, efficient operations, and cost controls.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:20.320
This can attract a wide consumer customer base and potentially deter new entrants from

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:21.320
the industry.

00:27:21.320 --> 00:27:23.960
Then we have differentiation.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:28.660
Differentiation aims to create a unique or distinctive product unlike others on the market.

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:34.460
And this can be achieved through unique product design, branding, or even customer service.

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:38.660
By offering something that's valuable and unique, companies can often charge premium

00:27:38.660 --> 00:27:41.500
prices and customers will be willing to pay.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:45.320
And then as we move to the bottom, there are the focus or niche strategies in the niche

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.420
market.

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:51.280
These strategies often involve concentrating efforts on a specific market or a specific

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:52.280
segment.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:56.440
This can be done either through cost focus or through differentiation focus, which means

00:27:56.440 --> 00:28:01.240
becoming the lowest cost leader in a niche and offering a uniquely tailored product in

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:02.880
a niche respectively.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:06.600
By focusing on smaller specialized markets, companies can build stronger relationships

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:11.080
with their customers and it's much easier to maintain these relationships.

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:15.280
By choosing one of these generic strategies, businesses can align their operations, marketing

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:22.980
efforts, and resource allocations to create a sustainable competitive advantage.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:27.440
So now we'll be looking at the three main financial statements that all companies need

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:30.820
to report or financial documents rather.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:34.600
So we have the statement of profit or loss, the statement of financial position and the

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:36.660
cash flow forecast.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:40.120
It's important to note that these documents do have different names that they're referred

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:45.200
to as I've indicated some of the popular other forms in parentheses.

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:49.320
So if you do go on the internet and you come across an income statement or profit or loss

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:52.380
statement, please do know that they're the same thing.

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:56.980
So each of these statements has a different purpose, which we'll be exploring soon.

00:28:56.980 --> 00:29:01.900
And when combined together, they provide an in-depth look at the through the lens of finances

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:07.780
and performance at how a company is performing and whether its stock is good.

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:12.020
And also keep in mind that all publicly traded companies need to report these three documents

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:16.280
to ensure transparency with the public and their investors, whereas private companies

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:18.700
don't need to report this.

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:23.580
Also note that in these documents, any negative numbers or losses will be represented with

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.580
parentheses around them.

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:27.780
In earlier examples, I use the color red.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:33.260
However, in financial documents, it's important to know all negative numbers are in parentheses.

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:35.180
All right.

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:38.140
So first we have the statement of profit or loss.

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:42.940
The statement of profit or loss is a financial statement that summarizes the revenues, costs,

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:45.940
and expenses of a company over a period of time.

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:50.020
So just to start with, all of these financial documents have this opening section.

00:29:50.020 --> 00:29:56.180
It will of course include the title, the company, and what type of financial document it is.

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.640
And more importantly, the period of time that is coming from.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:04.100
So in this example, we have the end of the year at whatever year.

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:05.100
Okay.

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:09.700
So the statement of profit or loss is also known as the statement of operations, statement

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:15.060
of financial results or income, the earning statement, the expense statement, income statement,

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:16.620
and so on.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:21.180
It's important to compare this profit and loss statement over time to see changes within

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:22.260
a company.

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:26.820
So this example, we only have it for one year, but investors typically look at several years

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:31.820
and compare it on a horizontal analysis, which we'll look at later, to see the growth of

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:34.080
a company over time.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:37.620
So whenever we're looking at this document, it's important to start from the top.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:43.740
So we look at the sales revenue, and as we go, we slowly reduce the total sales revenue

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:46.540
until we receive the retained profit.

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:52.540
So starting at costs of sales, this includes the cost of manufacturing goods and the costs

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:54.460
through which you would have to sell it.

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:56.980
This includes lighting at the store and so on.

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:58.820
This leads you to gross profit.

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:02.780
Then after expenses, you get your profits before interest in tax.

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:05.740
After paying interest, you get profits before tax.

00:31:05.740 --> 00:31:08.660
After paying your taxes, you get profit for your period.

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:12.220
So this is essentially all the surplus that a company has left over.

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:16.100
Then you pay your dividends to your shareholders, and this leads the retained profit for the

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:17.940
company.

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:22.080
So it's important to note that this is for a profit-making entity.

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:25.340
Even non-profit-making entities do report these statements.

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:31.420
However, in place of profit, they use the term surplus, since they aren't, well, for

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:35.500
profit, right?

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:37.780
So the statement of financial position.

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:42.600
This statement's a bit longer, so we'll look at it in two parts, assets and liabilities.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:46.580
But essentially this statement says, at the end of a financial period, where do you as

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.540
a company stand?

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.780
So the first half, the assets.

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:55.180
So again, looking from the top, we have the name of the company, title of the document,

00:31:55.180 --> 00:31:57.820
and the period that it's reporting for.

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:02.260
So in this section, we look at the assets, and assets are split into non-current and

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.340
current assets.

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:08.620
So non-current assets are assets that a company will be holding for a period greater than

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:09.620
a year.

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:13.460
This includes things like the factory that it's built on, any of the technical equipment

00:32:13.460 --> 00:32:15.580
they have, and so on.

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:21.140
Current assets, on the other hand, are assets that a company will hold for less than a period

00:32:21.140 --> 00:32:22.240
of a year.

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:26.480
This includes things like cash, debtors, and stock.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:31.340
So when you calculate the total value of non-current assets, you get 800 here, and current assets

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:33.260
is a 1100.

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:37.500
So your total assets add up to 1900.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:42.220
One thing that you may notice is accumulated depreciation, and it's in parentheses, and

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:43.900
it's a negative factor.

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:48.300
Although it's beyond the scope of this course, I'll give you a quick look at what depreciation

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:49.300
is.

00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:53.700
It's an accounting technique used by companies to reduce their overall assets, which can

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:56.420
help them in terms of tax breaks and so on.

00:32:56.420 --> 00:33:00.560
But yeah, when you add your non-current assets and your current assets, you get your total

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:03.380
assets of 1900.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:09.560
Now liabilities, again, liabilities are split into current liabilities and non-current liabilities.

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:12.900
Current liabilities are liabilities that a company will hold for a period less than a

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:13.900
year.

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:18.460
And non-current are liabilities that a company will hold for a period greater than a year.

00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:23.300
Things like bank overdraft, which are essentially drawing from a bank account until it gets

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.820
to a negative point, is a current liability.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:31.580
Trade predators is when a company is trading with another company, and the person you're

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:36.900
trading with allows you to pay later, essentially like an IOU.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.180
And we also have short-term loans, which will be paid back in a period less than a year.

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.860
And these add up to your total current liabilities.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:48.500
Other non-current liabilities are just long-term loans, which you would take out from the bank.

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:51.300
And these add up to your total liabilities.

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:54.780
Your net assets are your total assets minus your total liabilities.

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:58.540
So your total assets are 1900, and your total liabilities are 800.

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:03.940
So 1900 minus 800 gives you 1100, which is your net assets.

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:09.460
And an important calculation to know to see if you've done the financial position correct

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:13.740
is to take your assets and equate them to liabilities plus shareholder equity.

00:34:13.740 --> 00:34:16.980
If this comes out equal, then you've done it right.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:21.340
Shareholder equity is essentially the share capital, the value of your shares, and the

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:23.540
retained earnings of a company for the period.

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:27.420
So yeah, that's your statement of financial position.

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:29.340
And now we have the cash flow forecast.

00:34:29.340 --> 00:34:34.940
The cash flow forecast is for a shorter period of time, and it talks about all the money

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:40.500
going in and out of a company for, in this case, three months.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:45.540
So yeah, it allows advisors to assess the individual inflows and outflows of a company

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:49.060
to determine how important certain things in the company are and how expensive they

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:55.260
are and how they can be cut down to improve the total net cash flow of a company.

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:58.940
It also allows the company, it also allows for a month by month comparison to see the

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:02.180
growing effects of the company's operations.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:05.100
So as you see here in January, the opening balance is 800.

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:09.860
However, the total cash outflows, which are 306, are greater than the inflows, which are

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:10.860
300.

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:15.420
And as a result, the closing balance decreases by 6, 6 million.

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:17.620
So you have a closing balance of 2 million.

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:23.500
And we see this negative cash flow going over a period of three months, which allows us

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:29.100
to see that operating at this state for this company has negative effects and then suggests

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:33.220
that some outflows need to be decreased or inflows need to be increased.

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:39.580
Otherwise, companies may need to take out other, incorporate other forms of finances.

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:44.540
So the importance of these forms, these three forms, as I stated earlier, need to be made

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:47.220
public for publicly traded companies.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:53.140
It's important that companies ensure transparency with their investors and with the public so

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:58.540
that investors can make informed decisions whether they should invest in the company.

00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:03.180
These documents are also important for people within the company to ensure that to create

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:06.940
calculations like return on equity or ROE.

00:36:06.940 --> 00:36:12.020
And you can only do that by comparing across these documents or cross referencing.

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:17.660
So yeah, even now you can search up a certain publicly traded company and find these financial

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:18.980
documents on the internet.

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:23.860
However, you won't be able to do so with private companies.

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:27.340
So now we'll be looking at how to analyze these financial statements that we've just

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:28.700
discussed.

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:30.980
So there's three techniques that we can use.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:36.460
You can use ratios, a horizontal analysis, and a common size technique.

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:40.980
So first we'll go over ratios, which are, in my opinion, the simplest way and the quickest

00:36:40.980 --> 00:36:42.140
way to analyze.

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:48.620
So there's four types of ratios, profitability ratios, which measure your return on investment,

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:53.820
your liquidity ratios, which measure how easily you can move your money around, activity ratios,

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:58.660
which are representation of your day to day operations, and leverage ratios, which represent

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:02.740
how well you can utilize debt.

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:05.460
So we have profitability ratios.

00:37:05.460 --> 00:37:09.300
Profitability ratios are financial indicators used to evaluate a company's ability to generate

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:13.580
profits relative to its revenue, assets, and equity.

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:17.380
Key profitability ratios include the gross profit margin, as you can see on screen, which

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:22.260
measures profit after deducting production costs or cost of goods sold, and the net profit

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:26.700
margin, which assesses overall profit after all expenses.

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:30.500
We also have return on assets and return on equity, which are common ratios that show

00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:36.540
how efficiently a company generates profits from its assets and shareholder equity, respectively.

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:41.020
These ratios help investors and stakeholders gauge the company's profitability and its

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:44.340
effectiveness in turning revenue into earnings.

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.740
So as you can see, I have the formulas for each of these profitability ratios listed

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:54.940
on screen, and all of this information can obviously be found from three financial documents.

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:57.100
Next we have liquidity ratios.

00:37:57.100 --> 00:38:01.140
Liquidity ratios are a financial metrics that assess a company's ability to meet its short-term

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:02.940
financial obligations.

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:09.220
They measure the firm's ability to convert assets into cash quickly to recover or cover

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:11.660
immediate liabilities.

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:15.380
Common liquidity ratios include the current ratio, which divides the current assets by

00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:21.340
current liabilities, and the quick ratio, which excludes less liquid assets like inventory.

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:25.500
These ratios provide insights into a company's financial health and its capacity to handle

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:28.380
unexpected expenses or downturns.

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:33.220
High liquidity ratios generally indicate better short-term financial stability and flexibility,

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:39.900
however they aren't the ultimate analysis technique for liquidity.

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:47.220
Activity ratios are also known as asset utilization ratios, and they analyze how efficiently a

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:50.500
company manages to use its assets to generate revenue.

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:55.000
They provide insights into how well a company utilizes its resources and operations.

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:59.420
For example, we have inventory turnover, which measures how quickly inventory is sold and

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:04.140
replenished, and things like average collection period.

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:09.020
Activity ratios help businesses optimize their operations, manage working capital, and identify

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:13.500
areas where improvements can lead to increased productivity and profitability.

00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:17.100
Finally, we have debt leverage ratios.

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:21.740
Debt ratios are financial metrics that evaluate a company's level of debt in relation to its

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:23.520
equity or assets.

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:28.420
These ratios offer insights into a company's financial leverage and risk.

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:32.500
Common debt ratios include the debt-to-asset ratio, which measures debt relative to the

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:34.940
total assets a company has.

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:39.860
This ratio helps assess the company's ability to handle its debt obligations, and also provides

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:44.860
valuable information for investors and creditors regarding the firm's financial stability

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:47.940
and its transfer risk.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:52.300
Alright, so there's nuances to these ratios.

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:56.420
Although these ratios are super simple and easy to calculate, they aren't that useful

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:59.740
when you're comparing firms across industries.

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:02.300
So for example, we have Walmart and Honda.

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:08.060
If we take the example of the liquidity ratios with the quick ratio, you can't compare Honda

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:09.140
and Walmart.

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:13.100
It's likely that one of the companies will have very different styles of inventory, and

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.460
the way they process their equipment is very different.

00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:21.340
However, these ratios are useful when you're comparing companies within the same industry

00:40:21.340 --> 00:40:23.660
in the same position.

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:30.740
Alright, so now we can go through a quick simplified version of a consolidated income

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:35.500
statement and all these financial documents to calculate two ratios, the gross profit

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:38.780
margin and the net profit margin for Tesla.

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:42.940
So I got this data from the Wall Street Journal, and it's just a consolidated income statement

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:45.460
of Tesla with all this information here.

00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:49.900
So to calculate the gross profit margin, I have the formula right here.

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:53.780
So it's the revenues minus the cost of goods sold divided by the total revenues.

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:59.380
So using Excel, we can use the minus function to take the revenues, which is right here,

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:02.740
and subtract it from the cost of goods sold, which is right here.

00:41:02.740 --> 00:41:05.980
And this gives us 20,853.

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:11.740
And now we can use the divide function to divide this value by the total revenues, which

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:14.460
is the formula right here.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:20.100
And this gives us the gross profit margin ratio of 0.255, and so on.

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:22.700
And now we can do the same thing with the net profit margin.

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:23.700
But this is much more simple.

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:26.820
This is just the net income divided by revenues.

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:33.260
So we take the divide function, and we look for the net income, which is right here.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:38.140
And you take this, and you divide it by the revenues, which is right here again.

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:42.860
And using Excel, we can calculate it to be 0.15446.

00:41:42.860 --> 00:41:44.860
So this is a super simplified example.

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:49.020
The information on this Excel sheet is very basic.

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:52.060
In reality, when you're calculating some of the other ratios, you're going to need to

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:57.380
cross reference these financial documents, because lots of the information is located

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:02.220
on the cash flow forecast, some is reporting the income statement, and so on.

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:05.200
So as a result, it becomes a lot more complicated.

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:09.180
But if you have access to all the other files, which you should for publicly traded companies,

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:14.100
you'll be able to do these calculations no problem.

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:18.560
The next technique is horizontal analysis or trend analysis.

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:24.620
So horizontal analysis of financial statements involves comparing financial ratios and benchmarks

00:42:24.620 --> 00:42:28.140
or any item over a number of accounting periods.

00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:30.420
This method is also known as trend analysis.

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:35.700
So horizontal analysis allows the assessment of relative changes in different aspects of

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:39.340
the financial statements to be seen over time.

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:43.620
So let's look at this example.

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:48.700
One thing to notice when you're reading these statements over time is that the most recent

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:52.980
years represented on the left most column and the most and the oldest years on the right

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:57.100
most column, which kind of goes against our left to right reading style in English.

00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:59.500
So it's important to keep that in mind.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:04.780
But if we look at the numbers, we see a growth in the sales, a growth in the cost of sales,

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:07.060
a growth in profit and so on.

00:43:07.060 --> 00:43:08.820
So this can be calculated.

00:43:08.820 --> 00:43:14.800
So the change, the numerical change can be calculated and listed in this column.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:17.300
And the percentage change can be listed here.

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:22.340
The percentage change is calculated by taking the difference and dividing it by the overall

00:43:22.340 --> 00:43:23.340
amount.

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:27.820
So that's also called the year over year change.

00:43:27.820 --> 00:43:32.620
So returning to our consolidated income statement for Tesla that we saw in the previous example,

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:35.100
we can see the same changes happening over time.

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:39.740
So the leftmost column is the most recent year, which is 2022, and the rightmost column

00:43:39.740 --> 00:43:41.140
is 2018.

00:43:41.140 --> 00:43:45.600
So this records data for Tesla over the course of five years.

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:49.980
Keep in mind that some of this data happened during the COVID-19 pandemic, which may have

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:51.480
skewed results.

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:57.380
So that shows you that it's important to realize that macroeconomic effects like a pandemic

00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:01.000
or other industrial shocks can affect this data.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:05.020
But in large, it's not that big of a contribution, at least to Tesla.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:08.820
So if we look, we see a growth in sales revenue.

00:44:08.820 --> 00:44:18.820
So from 21,000, 24,000, 31,000, and then rapid growth from 2020 to 2022, with 20,000 growth

00:44:18.820 --> 00:44:21.740
and then 30,000 growth.

00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:26.900
At the same processes can be seen across various other factors or various other items.

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:31.100
And this goes to show you how this horizontal statement can be used to analyze growth over

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:34.300
time, even with Tesla.

00:44:34.300 --> 00:44:39.020
The last tool we'll be discussing in this section is common size analysis, which is

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:41.940
a financial state.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:46.380
The last technique we'll be discussing in this section is common size analysis.

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:50.800
Common size financial statements show each line item expressed as a percentage of a base

00:44:50.800 --> 00:44:54.980
figure within the statement, and it's used for vertical analysis.

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:58.860
So common size financial statements help to analyze and compare a company's performance

00:44:58.860 --> 00:45:00.680
over several periods.

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:05.780
The common size percentages can subsequently be used to compare those of competitors and

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:09.780
determine how a company is performing relative to the rest of the industry.

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:14.340
A common size income statement is an income statement in which each line item is expressed

00:45:14.340 --> 00:45:17.740
as a percentage of the value of revenue or sales.

00:45:17.740 --> 00:45:21.700
A common size balance sheet is a balance sheet that displays both a numeric value and relative

00:45:21.700 --> 00:45:23.860
percentage for total assets.

00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:28.300
So in the following example, we'll be looking over a common size statement for an income

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:31.300
statement or balance sheet.

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:35.900
Okay, so here we have a generic example of what one would look like.

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:40.900
As you can see, we have the normal side of a balance sheet or a statement of financial

00:45:40.900 --> 00:45:46.820
position, except one more column is added that expresses everything as percentages.

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:51.100
In this example, you would think percentages aren't too helpful, and you'd be right.

00:45:51.100 --> 00:45:53.660
And that's because the numbers here are very straightforward.

00:45:53.660 --> 00:45:57.220
The total assets are 100,000, which makes 100%.

00:45:57.220 --> 00:45:59.220
And all these numbers are very straightforward.

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:04.100
However, in a situation where numbers are much more miscellaneous and intricate, these

00:46:04.100 --> 00:46:08.420
percentages help to clarify and watch more information.

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:12.780
Here this is extremely helpful because it can show us what percent of our assets, for

00:46:12.780 --> 00:46:15.420
example, our current assets are in which form.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:19.300
So in this example, for our current assets, the majority is in the form of total cash

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:23.500
and inventory, whereas the rest is in the form of prepaid expenses and accounts receivables.

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:30.140
This can suggest that the company may need to invest its total cash into other assets

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:35.100
that can generate revenue, and it may have an inventory issue, turning inventory into

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:37.940
actual revenue.

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:41.420
And if we look at our non-current assets, the majority of our assets are in our plan,

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:44.260
property and equipment, whereas very little is in our goodwill.

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:48.820
This can help us identify what value certain parts of our business bring and what parts

00:46:48.820 --> 00:46:50.540
aren't as helpful.

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:55.340
So in the non-current example, we see how we have so much value in our equipment, our

00:46:55.340 --> 00:46:59.780
property, and our plant, whereas the brand name and value, which is goodwill, isn't

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:01.700
that strong.

00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:05.660
This can also help us identify which parts of our liabilities are the greatest and which

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:10.060
parts are the weakest, and how we can take future steps to address these and prevent

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:12.620
any other issues from rising.

00:47:12.620 --> 00:47:16.980
This same thing can be seen for other forms of statements like the income statement,

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:22.980
which I mentioned earlier, but it's essentially the same thing just in another financial statement.

00:47:22.980 --> 00:47:25.340
All right.

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:30.680
So let's say you have the opportunity to install solar panels on your house.

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:35.980
The solar panel company says it's going to cost you $10,000 to install solar panels.

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:39.260
But they say in the long run, you're going to be able to save money.

00:47:39.260 --> 00:47:40.820
Should you make the investment?

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:44.620
Well, in this class, we're going to be in this section, we're going to be talking about

00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:46.700
capital budgeting.

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:48.740
So what is capital budgeting?

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:49.980
Why do we do it?

00:47:49.980 --> 00:47:51.580
And how is it done?

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:55.100
And with these tools, we can answer whether we should make the investment for the solar

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:56.460
panels.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:59.200
So first, what is capital budgeting?

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:03.420
Capital budgeting is the process of evaluating and selecting long term investment projects

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:07.460
like solar panels that involve significant financial outlays.

00:48:07.460 --> 00:48:12.340
It's a decision making processes for businesses and individuals to determine which projects

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:17.380
are worth pursuing based on their potential to generate returns or benefits over an extended

00:48:17.380 --> 00:48:18.940
period.

00:48:18.940 --> 00:48:22.980
Capital budgeting helps organizations and individuals allocate their financial resources

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:29.380
effectively, maximizing the value of investments and minimizing the risk of making poor choices.

00:48:29.380 --> 00:48:33.460
It's a systematic approach that considers not only the immediate costs and returns,

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:38.620
but also the long term impact on financial performance and overall strategic goals of

00:48:38.620 --> 00:48:41.100
an individual or business.

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:46.900
Okay, so first of all, why do companies even make investments in fixed assets?

00:48:46.900 --> 00:48:53.060
Well, investments can increase capacity, they can overcome some regulations, they are innovative,

00:48:53.060 --> 00:48:55.580
which can give companies a unique advantage.

00:48:55.580 --> 00:48:58.500
And there's so many other reasons.

00:48:58.500 --> 00:49:02.500
But why is capital budgeting important for valuation?

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:06.820
Capital budgeting is important for several reasons, but I'll give you three main reasons.

00:49:06.820 --> 00:49:09.380
First of all, it helps the resource allocation.

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:13.880
There's limited financial resources and capital budgeting can help ensure companies focus

00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:16.340
on the most promising investments.

00:49:16.340 --> 00:49:18.780
It also helps for long term planning.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:22.340
This is because it allows companies to plan for the future by considering the effects of

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:26.940
their investment using tools that we'll discuss later, over an extended period and manage

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:29.580
risk while maximizing returns.

00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:33.600
And it also considers the time value of money, again, which we'll discuss further.

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:37.900
But capital budgeting considers the time value of money, recognizing that a dollar today

00:49:37.900 --> 00:49:40.700
is worth more than a dollar in the future.

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:44.860
This also ensures that cash flow predictions are properly discounted.

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:48.740
Okay, so how do companies pay for investments?

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:54.860
They pay for it in three ways using cash flow, debt, and equity.

00:49:54.860 --> 00:49:59.340
So before we continue, there's two more concepts you need to understand before we're able to

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:02.940
discuss capital budgeting and how to actually consider it.

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:07.180
Internal Rate of Return, or IRR for short, and Cost of Capital.

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:12.100
The Internal Rate of Return, or IRR, is a finance term that helps us assess how lucrative

00:50:12.100 --> 00:50:14.140
an investment or project could be.

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:18.540
IRR is the annual growth that an investment is expected to make.

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:23.340
If the IRR is higher than what you could earn from a safe investment, like a bank deposit

00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:28.100
or investing in some safe stocks, then the project might be worth the risk.

00:50:28.100 --> 00:50:34.380
In short, IRR helps us decide if a business idea is a good investment.

00:50:34.380 --> 00:50:36.580
So the steps of capital budgeting.

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:39.540
First, a project proposal is developed.

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:44.820
This involves either individuals or businesses looking at an opportunity to make an investment

00:50:44.820 --> 00:50:46.900
and thinking it's a good idea.

00:50:46.900 --> 00:50:52.280
Then management reviews and prioritizes projects based on their economic viability.

00:50:52.280 --> 00:50:56.340
So after looking at the potential cash flow projections, management will either decide

00:50:56.340 --> 00:51:01.820
if an investment is positive or negative for the company and thus decide to prioritize

00:51:01.820 --> 00:51:03.220
or ignore it.

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:07.260
Then funds are allocated on these projects to ensure the development and the results

00:51:07.260 --> 00:51:10.060
are tracked to see if the projections are accurate.

00:51:10.060 --> 00:51:16.820
If projections are inaccurate, funds are either removed, reduced, or increased.

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:21.740
After the investment is completed, companies can reflect on the process and decide whether

00:51:21.740 --> 00:51:25.420
future similar investments should be made.

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:28.520
So Capital Cash Flows.

00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:34.020
The value of an initial investment is equal to the cost of the new asset plus the installation's

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:40.980
cost associated with the new asset minus the after-tax proceeds from selling the old asset.

00:51:40.980 --> 00:51:45.460
Keep in mind the after-tax proceeds from selling an old asset is equal to the sale price minus

00:51:45.460 --> 00:51:49.280
the tax on capital gain, which is a bit advanced from this course.

00:51:49.280 --> 00:51:52.900
But all you need to understand is that that is considered when calculating the price of

00:51:52.900 --> 00:51:57.160
the initial investment.

00:51:57.160 --> 00:52:00.580
So capital budgeting results in changes in working capital.

00:52:00.580 --> 00:52:04.460
If a current asset increases in value, it's a use of working capital.

00:52:04.460 --> 00:52:08.160
If current liabilities increase, it's a source of working capital.

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:14.060
That means current assets are cash, or inventory, which are uses of capital, and liabilities

00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:18.100
are things like loans, which are sources of working capital.

00:52:18.100 --> 00:52:22.740
An increase in networking capital means it's a source of cash for the company, and a decrease

00:52:22.740 --> 00:52:25.520
is a use of cash.

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:30.420
So capital budgeting are the three main methods that we use to decide whether we should invest

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:34.500
our payback period, which we'll discuss next, net present value, which we've discussed

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:38.140
earlier, and IRR, which we've all just discussed.

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:43.140
So payback period, it's a very basic concept, and it essentially discusses how long it takes

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:47.820
for a company to recoup its investments on a fixed asset.

00:52:47.820 --> 00:52:51.180
So in this example, we have a Ford factory.

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:54.900
Initially it costs $5,000 to invest in this Ford factory.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:59.260
However, as the years go on, it generates positive cash flows.

00:52:59.260 --> 00:53:04.420
So payback period essentially looks at how long it takes for the company to generate,

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:07.260
to receive a net positive cash flow.

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:11.860
And as you can see, initially the cash flows, the cumulative cash flow is negative until

00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:14.660
year four, when it finally breaks positive.

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:18.940
Okay, so now let's return back to our example.

00:53:18.940 --> 00:53:21.620
Our solar panel costs $10,000.

00:53:21.620 --> 00:53:24.020
Should we make the investment?

00:53:24.020 --> 00:53:28.220
So using capital budgeting techniques, we can predict the cash flows.

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:35.140
So the cash flows over the years are $2,000, $2,500, $3,500, $4,000, and $4,500, with an

00:53:35.140 --> 00:53:37.060
initial cost of $10,000.

00:53:37.060 --> 00:53:38.900
Should we install these panels?

00:53:38.900 --> 00:53:44.260
Well, if you look at the cost of capital, which is 12%, that means any other investment

00:53:44.260 --> 00:53:49.860
we could invest in would generate $1,200 in net present value.

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:55.820
If our investment on the solar panels is greater than our other cost of capital, then it's

00:53:55.820 --> 00:53:57.060
a good investment.

00:53:57.060 --> 00:54:00.980
So using discounting our cash flows, we can find a value.

00:54:00.980 --> 00:54:06.220
We find that the value of the solar panels, the net present value of the solar panels

00:54:06.220 --> 00:54:15.440
is $1,219, which is greater than the value of, which is greater than our cost of capital.

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:20.940
So here we can see how we calculate our net present value and how it shifts based on our

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:23.780
discount rate and our cash flows.

00:54:23.780 --> 00:54:29.700
So this Excel function is set up so that the discount rate is called percent, which is

00:54:29.700 --> 00:54:33.580
our cost of capital, and our cash flows are accounted for.

00:54:33.580 --> 00:54:39.580
And you can do this by typing equals NPV, selecting the discount, comma, and selecting

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:44.140
our cash flows, which yields the same number here and here.

00:54:44.140 --> 00:54:49.260
So if we wanted to adjust our factors to see how it would be in different scenarios, we

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:52.380
can change these numbers and see how it would work.

00:54:52.380 --> 00:54:57.820
So if our discount rate was instead of 12%, 20%, you can see how our net present value

00:54:57.820 --> 00:54:59.380
has decreased.

00:54:59.380 --> 00:55:04.940
This suggests that other investments which have higher, which are better cost of capital

00:55:04.940 --> 00:55:07.940
could have been a better investment than investing in these solar panels.

00:55:07.940 --> 00:55:13.840
However, if we decrease our cost of capital to something like 5%, we see the net present

00:55:13.840 --> 00:55:16.220
value increases even greater.

00:55:16.220 --> 00:55:21.780
This shows how different costs of capital can affect the net present value of our investment

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:24.540
by comparing alternative investments.

00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:29.260
Additionally, we can look at how cash flow affects the net present value.

00:55:29.260 --> 00:55:33.540
If our initial cash flow is 20,000 instead of 10,000, thus essentially making the cost

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:37.340
was double, our net present value decreases significantly.

00:55:37.340 --> 00:55:42.380
This shows how the price of investment can also affect the value.

00:55:42.380 --> 00:55:49.260
And that increasing our cash flows significantly increases our net present value.

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:54.420
Alright, in this section, we'll be talking about macroeconomics.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:57.980
So first of all, what is macroeconomics?

00:55:57.980 --> 00:56:01.660
Macroeconomics is a branch of economics that studies the overall behavior of an economy

00:56:01.660 --> 00:56:07.380
focusing on large scale factors like economic growth, inflation, unemployment, and national

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:08.700
income.

00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:13.780
It examines how policies and events impact the economy as a whole rather than individuals

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:17.780
such as individual businesses or individual markets.

00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:23.140
Some of the key factors in macroeconomics are the size of the economy, economic growth,

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:28.980
government policy, fiscal policy, monetary policy, inflation, and international trade.

00:56:28.980 --> 00:56:31.900
Most of these which we'll touch upon.

00:56:31.900 --> 00:56:35.620
So one thing that we should understand is the business cycle.

00:56:35.620 --> 00:56:40.500
The business cycle is very intertwined with economics.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:41.880
Everything is cyclical.

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:47.700
So the business cycle, also known as the economic cycle, is the recurring pattern of fluctuations

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:50.700
and an economy's overall activity over time.

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:56.220
It can be divided into four main phases, as you guys can see in this diagram.

00:56:56.220 --> 00:56:59.460
In this example, it starts with the trough or the lowest point.

00:56:59.460 --> 00:57:02.660
So the trough is the lowest point in the business cycle.

00:57:02.660 --> 00:57:08.980
At this point, economic activity is at its lowest and economic conditions are the worst.

00:57:08.980 --> 00:57:14.740
However, at this point, the economy sets the stage for a potential turnaround as it moves

00:57:14.740 --> 00:57:18.780
into the expansion stage.

00:57:18.780 --> 00:57:23.560
Some of the features of this stage is monetary and fiscal policy employed by the central

00:57:23.560 --> 00:57:29.740
banks and government, which we'll touch upon later, starting to rise in economic growth,

00:57:29.740 --> 00:57:36.300
an increase in employment and manufacturing from this trough, and a gradual shift towards

00:57:36.300 --> 00:57:38.740
increases in profits.

00:57:38.740 --> 00:57:40.940
Next we have the expansion phase.

00:57:40.940 --> 00:57:45.460
During this phase, economic activity such as production, employment, and consumer spending

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:48.820
is on the rise, as set by the trough.

00:57:48.820 --> 00:57:52.460
Businesses are expanding and there's a general sense of optimism and an increase in consumer

00:57:52.460 --> 00:57:54.780
confidence throughout the economy.

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:59.380
Growth is driven by an increase in consumer demand, investment, and also by monetary and

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:01.240
fiscal policy.

00:58:01.240 --> 00:58:06.040
As you can see, the effects of fiscal and monetary policy are starting to benefit, however,

00:58:06.040 --> 00:58:11.180
the governments and central banks are drawing back on their intervention.

00:58:11.180 --> 00:58:17.280
Economic growth is also still positive, however, it's not as rapid as it was during the trough.

00:58:17.280 --> 00:58:22.860
At this stage, companies are still hiring and manufacturing activity is ongoing and

00:58:22.860 --> 00:58:26.100
margins of companies also start to increase.

00:58:26.100 --> 00:58:27.780
Then we reach the peak.

00:58:27.780 --> 00:58:31.700
The peak is the highest point of economic activity in the business cycle.

00:58:31.700 --> 00:58:37.140
At this stage, growth begins to slow and even starts to decrease, and the economy reaches

00:58:37.140 --> 00:58:39.100
a state of maximum output.

00:58:39.100 --> 00:58:44.080
However, signs of potential inflationary pressures may begin to emerge.

00:58:44.080 --> 00:58:48.060
At this point, fiscal and monetary policies employed by central banks and governments

00:58:48.060 --> 00:58:52.180
may go in the opposite direction to prevent too much growth, which is inflation.

00:58:52.180 --> 00:58:57.080
Additionally, economic growth stagnates and employers have trouble finding workers and

00:58:57.080 --> 00:58:58.900
wages increase in value.

00:58:58.900 --> 00:59:00.700
This is an effect of inflation.

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:05.100
Additionally, earnings start to disappoint workers against high expectations due to economic

00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:06.700
growth.

00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:09.340
This leads into a recession or a contraction.

00:59:09.340 --> 00:59:15.060
So following the peak, the economy enters this contractionary phase.

00:59:15.060 --> 00:59:19.100
Economic indicators like GDP, employment, and consumer spending, which we touched upon

00:59:19.100 --> 00:59:21.420
earlier, start to decline.

00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:26.660
Businesses cut down on production, start laying off employees, and this may result in a decrease

00:59:26.660 --> 00:59:28.500
in consumer confidence.

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:35.220
This phase is commonly referred to as a recession, but this term varies depending on the severity

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.300
and duration of this period.

00:59:38.300 --> 00:59:41.980
Business cycle is driven by various factors, including shifts in consumer and investor

00:59:41.980 --> 00:59:45.940
sentiments, increased technology, and changes to monetary and fiscal policies.

00:59:45.940 --> 00:59:50.580
However, it is continuous and it always happens.

00:59:50.580 --> 00:59:55.700
However, one thing to note is that in this graph, we see the business cycle as reaching

00:59:55.700 --> 00:59:58.100
the trough again, right?

00:59:58.100 --> 01:00:02.820
But one thing to notice is that the economy is constantly growing, so it's on an upward

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:03.820
trend.

01:00:03.820 --> 01:00:08.660
However, these cycles go throughout the upward trend, if that makes sense.

01:00:08.660 --> 01:00:13.560
If you look closely here, the trough in the original early cycle is a bit lower than the

01:00:13.560 --> 01:00:16.060
trough after we reached the recession.

01:00:16.060 --> 01:00:21.300
This shows that the economy is growing, however, while it's growing, it is experiencing cycles.

01:00:21.300 --> 01:00:25.860
Okay, so the key factors of the macro-economy that we'll be touching upon.

01:00:25.860 --> 01:00:31.860
GDP, or gross domestic product, unemployment slash unemployment, inflation, and interest

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:34.500
rates, which we've already discussed.

01:00:34.500 --> 01:00:36.300
So first, what is GDP?

01:00:36.300 --> 01:00:41.940
GDP, or the gross domestic product of a company, represents the total cost of goods being sold

01:00:41.940 --> 01:00:46.340
in that country or in that nation in a period, typically one year.

01:00:46.340 --> 01:00:52.700
So GDP is calculated as consumer spending, which is C, investments made, which is I,

01:00:52.700 --> 01:00:57.060
government spending, which is G, and the difference between exports and imports, which is represented

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:58.180
as X minus M.

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:06.340
Okay, something to note about industries and the business cycles.

01:01:06.340 --> 01:01:12.180
Certain industries and companies are divided into categories called cyclical or defensive

01:01:12.180 --> 01:01:14.060
categories.

01:01:14.060 --> 01:01:18.060
Cyclical industries are industries which face the repercussions of the business cycle.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:22.700
During certain times, they will be in an upward boom, and during certain times, they may not

01:01:22.700 --> 01:01:25.540
be able to operate at all.

01:01:25.540 --> 01:01:31.700
Examples of this include the hotel industry, resorts, dining, and any other excessive industries

01:01:31.700 --> 01:01:35.740
that are created by consumer wants.

01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:38.980
Defensive industries, on the other hand, are things that aren't too heavily affected by

01:01:38.980 --> 01:01:39.980
the business cycle.

01:01:39.980 --> 01:01:43.340
This is because they're necessary for human operation.

01:01:43.340 --> 01:01:48.300
Things like this include health services, utilities, health technology, and so on.

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:53.260
However, some industries are in between cyclical and defensive.

01:01:53.260 --> 01:01:58.700
For example, consumer services are somewhat cyclical and somewhat defensive.

01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:04.740
For example, things like accommodation are cyclical and defensive to some extent.

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:09.940
Some houses in certain areas, which may be seen as luxury, may be cyclical when it comes

01:02:09.940 --> 01:02:11.820
to renting or on Airbnb.

01:02:11.820 --> 01:02:18.260
However, basic accommodation is defensive since humans need it at all times.

01:02:18.260 --> 01:02:20.460
So first, unemployment.

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:26.780
So unemployment is split into three categories, cyclical, structural, and frictional.

01:02:26.780 --> 01:02:29.060
So what is cyclical unemployment?

01:02:29.060 --> 01:02:32.740
Cyclical unemployment results from fluctuations in the overall economy.

01:02:32.740 --> 01:02:37.620
When the economy enters a recession or a downturn, demand for goods and services decreases, causing

01:02:37.620 --> 01:02:40.280
businesses to scale back production.

01:02:40.280 --> 01:02:44.740
As a result, they lay off workers leading to cyclical unemployment.

01:02:44.740 --> 01:02:46.740
Consider the construction industry.

01:02:46.740 --> 01:02:51.520
During an economic boom, there's a high demand for new homes and infrastructural projects.

01:02:51.520 --> 01:02:54.460
Construction companies hire more workers to meet this growing demand.

01:02:54.460 --> 01:02:59.300
However, when a recession hits, the demand for new projects drops, causing these companies

01:02:59.300 --> 01:03:03.260
to cut back on their workforce, resulting in cyclical unemployment for construction

01:03:03.260 --> 01:03:04.420
workers.

01:03:04.420 --> 01:03:09.180
Similarly, in the tourism sector, when the economy is thriving, people have more disposable

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:11.260
income to travel and explore.

01:03:11.260 --> 01:03:15.680
This leads to increased demand for flights, hotels, entertainment like movies, and so

01:03:15.680 --> 01:03:16.680
on.

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:21.940
Yet, in an economic downturn, people tighten their budgets, leading to reduced travel and

01:03:21.940 --> 01:03:24.420
low demand for these tourism-related services.

01:03:24.420 --> 01:03:28.340
And as a result, there are losses in jobs in this industry.

01:03:28.340 --> 01:03:32.460
In both examples, cyclical unemployment is closely tied to the fluctuations in the business

01:03:32.460 --> 01:03:34.380
cycle which we saw earlier.

01:03:34.380 --> 01:03:39.140
As the economy contracts and expands, industries that heavily rely on consumer spending and

01:03:39.140 --> 01:03:46.900
investments can experience shifts in employment levels due to the changes in demand.

01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:49.060
Next we have structural unemployment.

01:03:49.060 --> 01:03:53.500
Structural unemployment arises from a mismatch between the skills and qualifications of job

01:03:53.500 --> 01:03:56.840
seekers and the requirements of available jobs.

01:03:56.840 --> 01:04:01.180
This mismatch is often caused by changes in the economy, such as due to technology or

01:04:01.180 --> 01:04:06.940
shifts in consumer preferences, making certain skills obsolete or less in demand.

01:04:06.940 --> 01:04:10.660
For instance, let's consider the decline of the typewriter industry with the advent

01:04:10.660 --> 01:04:12.100
of computers.

01:04:12.100 --> 01:04:16.340
Skill typists, who were very proficient at using the typewriter, might have found themselves

01:04:16.340 --> 01:04:20.620
structurally unemployed as the demand for their specific skillset, efficient use of

01:04:20.620 --> 01:04:24.300
the typewriter, dwindled due to technological advancements.

01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:28.220
This is because computers were much more efficient, much more accessible, and people could do

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:33.140
the same job that typewriters could do at home by themselves.

01:04:33.140 --> 01:04:37.980
Another example could be seen in the decline of manufacturing jobs in certain regions.

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:42.380
As manufacturing processes became more automated, companies may require fewer workers on the

01:04:42.380 --> 01:04:44.180
factory floor.

01:04:44.180 --> 01:04:47.740
This can lead to structural unemployment as individuals with specific skills to manual

01:04:47.740 --> 01:04:52.860
assembly or machine operations face challenges of finding relevant employment.

01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:57.040
In the modern context, rapid advancements in artificial intelligence and automation

01:04:57.040 --> 01:05:01.200
could potentially lead to structural employment for certain administrative roles which do

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:04.020
work that AI can easily replace.

01:05:04.020 --> 01:05:09.220
These include tasks like data entry or customer service, which AI, even at its current state,

01:05:09.220 --> 01:05:14.060
can likely handle, rendering these traditional jobs unnecessary.

01:05:14.060 --> 01:05:18.260
Structural unemployment underscores the importance of continuous skill development and adaptation

01:05:18.260 --> 01:05:20.420
to changing job markets.

01:05:20.420 --> 01:05:24.820
Individuals need to retain and acquire new skills to remain competitive in an evolving

01:05:24.820 --> 01:05:29.140
landscape and minimize the impact of structural shifts in the economy.

01:05:29.140 --> 01:05:32.460
Lastly, we have frictional unemployment.

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:37.660
Frictional unemployment is likely the type of unemployment that most people will experience.

01:05:37.660 --> 01:05:41.340
It occurs when individuals are transitioning between jobs or entering the workforce for

01:05:41.340 --> 01:05:42.340
the first time.

01:05:42.340 --> 01:05:47.600
It's a natural and unavoidable aspect of a dynamic job market, where people are constantly

01:05:47.600 --> 01:05:52.820
seeking better, higher-paying jobs with better benefits, while employers are looking for

01:05:52.820 --> 01:05:55.060
better and better candidates.

01:05:55.060 --> 01:05:58.060
Imagine a recent college graduate entering the job market.

01:05:58.060 --> 01:06:02.500
They might experience frictional unemployment as they search for their first full-time position.

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:05.860
During this time, they're looking for a job that aligns with their skills, interests,

01:06:05.860 --> 01:06:09.960
career goals, pay, and maybe it's even close to their house.

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:14.720
This process of searching and applying and interviewing and negotiating tasks takes time

01:06:14.720 --> 01:06:19.500
and can result in temporary unemployment, which we refer to as frictional unemployment.

01:06:19.500 --> 01:06:23.580
Another example is when someone voluntarily leaves a job to pursue a new opportunity.

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:27.140
They may experience frictional unemployment during the gap between leaving one job and

01:06:27.140 --> 01:06:28.860
starting their next job.

01:06:28.860 --> 01:06:32.980
This gap is often necessary to handle the logistics of transitioning, such as relocating

01:06:32.980 --> 01:06:35.700
or completing necessary paperwork.

01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:39.780
Frictional unemployment, while inevitable, can be reduced through effective job search

01:06:39.780 --> 01:06:44.180
strategies, networking, and the use of online job platforms.

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:48.340
It's important to note that frictional unemployment is generally considered to be a healthy aspect

01:06:48.340 --> 01:06:54.580
of a dynamic job market, as it reflects the individual's ability to move and their flexibility

01:06:54.580 --> 01:06:59.820
in pursuing opportunities that best fit their skill sets and aspirations.

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:02.020
So, inflation.

01:07:02.020 --> 01:07:07.180
When we're calculating GDP, as we learned earlier, the value of money changes over time.

01:07:07.180 --> 01:07:11.380
Money in the future is not worth the same as it is right now.

01:07:11.380 --> 01:07:17.580
As a result, GDP numbers without being deflated could show consistent growth, whereas in reality,

01:07:17.580 --> 01:07:22.380
the real GDP, which is what we refer to GDP with the time value of money taken into account,

01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:23.900
could be decreasing.

01:07:23.900 --> 01:07:28.020
As a result, to calculate the real GDP of a country, we take the nominal GDP, which

01:07:28.020 --> 01:07:33.140
is what would be reported, and we take away the impact of inflation.

01:07:33.140 --> 01:07:40.500
Okay, so who is really playing a role in the macroeconomy?

01:07:40.500 --> 01:07:46.340
So, the two main players that control it are the governments and central banks, which implement

01:07:46.340 --> 01:07:48.380
and develop policies.

01:07:48.380 --> 01:07:53.620
And as a result, companies and consumers are impacted by these changes and need to react.

01:07:53.620 --> 01:08:01.300
So, governments implement fiscal policy, whereas banks implement monetary policy.

01:08:01.300 --> 01:08:05.340
So, first, what is monetary policy?

01:08:05.340 --> 01:08:09.340
Monetary policy involves a bank's management of a country's money supply and interest

01:08:09.340 --> 01:08:12.220
rates to influence economic activity.

01:08:12.220 --> 01:08:16.620
By adjusting interest rates and employing tools like open market operations, central

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:22.020
banks can control inflation, encourage borrowing, and spending during slow economic periods

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:27.900
using expansionary monetary policy, or cool down an overheating economy using a contractionary

01:08:27.900 --> 01:08:30.180
monetary policy.

01:08:30.180 --> 01:08:34.700
Expansionary monetary policy is when central banks reduce interest rates to make borrowing

01:08:34.700 --> 01:08:36.580
money easier for consumers.

01:08:36.580 --> 01:08:40.700
This is because the interest they would have to pay on a loan reduces, taking the loan

01:08:40.700 --> 01:08:42.780
much more appealing.

01:08:42.780 --> 01:08:46.340
Contractionary monetary policy, on the other hand, is when central banks increase interest

01:08:46.340 --> 01:08:52.860
rates to make borrowing more expensive, causing borrowers to pay more interest on their loans,

01:08:52.860 --> 01:08:57.500
decreasing the appeal of these loans.

01:08:57.500 --> 01:09:01.740
An interesting example of monetary policy in action is Japan's use of interest rates

01:09:01.740 --> 01:09:03.460
to stimulate the economy.

01:09:03.460 --> 01:09:09.900
Typically, in a country that is using expansionary monetary policy, interest rates could be lowered

01:09:09.900 --> 01:09:14.260
from 3% to 2%, for example, to encourage borrowing.

01:09:14.260 --> 01:09:17.980
Japan, however, decided to take an unconventional approach.

01:09:17.980 --> 01:09:22.020
The Bank of Japan set an interest rate of negative 0.1.

01:09:22.020 --> 01:09:26.420
This is extremely unique and a choice made by Japan's government to meet their specific

01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:27.580
needs.

01:09:27.580 --> 01:09:31.980
This was their attempt to overcome deflation, which was a big issue in Japan.

01:09:31.980 --> 01:09:36.940
By making interest rates negative, it makes it more economical to borrow and spend money

01:09:36.940 --> 01:09:38.740
than just holding on to it.

01:09:38.740 --> 01:09:43.420
As a result, people kept taking on loans because it was generating that money, and as a result,

01:09:43.420 --> 01:09:51.020
they started spending more, which stimulated Japan's economy.

01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:52.860
Now fiscal policy.

01:09:52.860 --> 01:09:58.060
Fiscal policy involves how governments use taxes and spending to influence the country's

01:09:58.060 --> 01:09:59.060
economy.

01:09:59.060 --> 01:10:03.440
By adjusting tax rates and government spending levels, governments aim to stimulate the economic

01:10:03.440 --> 01:10:08.620
growth during downturns, using expansionary fiscal policy, or control inflation during

01:10:08.620 --> 01:10:12.100
periods of high growth, using contractionary fiscal policy.

01:10:12.100 --> 01:10:16.340
So expansionary fiscal policy, similar to expansionary monetary policy, is when governments

01:10:16.340 --> 01:10:21.380
spend more money to increase the circulation of money within the circular flow of income,

01:10:21.380 --> 01:10:23.940
which creates jobs and more.

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:27.800
Contractory fiscal policy is when governments increase interest rates to decrease the disposable

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:30.820
income of people to reduce their spendings.

01:10:30.820 --> 01:10:35.980
An example of how governments can spend their money is through investments in military,

01:10:35.980 --> 01:10:40.600
investments in infrastructure, or investments in social programs, which stimulate the economy,

01:10:40.600 --> 01:10:42.920
and can be called injections.

01:10:42.920 --> 01:10:46.500
The ways they can influence tax rates are through increases or decreases in corporate

01:10:46.500 --> 01:10:51.980
tax, and increases or decreases in individual tax, like income rates.

01:10:51.980 --> 01:10:56.460
Fiscal policy, as we mentioned earlier, can be expansionary to promote spending, or contractionary

01:10:56.460 --> 01:10:58.500
to reduce spending.

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:02.500
As you can assume, monetary policy, which is influenced by the central bank, is much

01:11:02.500 --> 01:11:05.260
more fast-acting than fiscal policy.

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:09.820
Controlling interest rates is much faster than starting infrastructural projects, which can

01:11:09.820 --> 01:11:12.340
take years to see the effects of.

01:11:12.340 --> 01:11:16.900
Because interest rates can directly affect money circulation, they affect much faster.

01:11:16.900 --> 01:11:21.660
Whereas things like infrastructural projects, their benefits come from increased employment

01:11:21.660 --> 01:11:26.700
and injections into the economy in the form of available buildings where companies can

01:11:26.700 --> 01:11:27.860
relocate.

01:11:27.860 --> 01:11:32.660
This often takes time for companies to find these locations to relocate, and additionally,

01:11:32.660 --> 01:11:39.100
employment doesn't immediately increase the economy.

01:11:39.100 --> 01:11:44.220
In this section, we'll be going over ESG, or environment, social, and governance for

01:11:44.220 --> 01:11:45.220
short.

01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:50.180
ESG has become a topic of much concern in recent years especially, but has existed for

01:11:50.180 --> 01:11:52.700
hundreds of years and even beyond that.

01:11:52.700 --> 01:11:58.220
ESG is the social mission of a company, and integrating social aspects and objectives

01:11:58.220 --> 01:12:00.420
within a company's goals.

01:12:00.420 --> 01:12:05.580
However, there is an increasing demand for ESG in the investing process from investors

01:12:05.580 --> 01:12:06.900
and stakeholders alike.

01:12:06.900 --> 01:12:13.300
However, there is so much skepticism regarding the role of ESG and responsible investing.

01:12:13.300 --> 01:12:17.300
And the idea that focusing on ESG means compromising returns.

01:12:17.300 --> 01:12:19.480
However, this isn't the case.

01:12:19.480 --> 01:12:24.660
In this section, we'll be going over what ESG is, why it's so important, and how investors

01:12:24.660 --> 01:12:30.380
should take into consideration ESG not only advance social goals, but to ensure that their

01:12:30.380 --> 01:12:32.500
portfolios are profitable.

01:12:32.500 --> 01:12:35.420
Alright, so first, what is ESG?

01:12:35.420 --> 01:12:40.300
ESG, which stands for Environmental, Social, and Governments, is a comprehensive framework

01:12:40.300 --> 01:12:45.080
that evaluates a company's performance and practices in three key areas.

01:12:45.080 --> 01:12:48.820
The environmental aspect focuses on how a company interacts with the planet, from how

01:12:48.820 --> 01:12:54.860
it goes about carbon emissions, to managing resources, to addressing waste and pollution.

01:12:54.860 --> 01:12:59.420
The social aspect revolves around relationships, including how a company treats its employees,

01:12:59.420 --> 01:13:04.860
fosters diversity and inclusion, and engages with local communities through initiatives

01:13:04.860 --> 01:13:07.180
and philanthropy, and CSR.

01:13:07.180 --> 01:13:11.740
Finally, the governance aspect examines a company's internal structure, ethics, and

01:13:11.740 --> 01:13:12.980
accountability.

01:13:12.980 --> 01:13:18.180
This encompasses everything, from the composition of the board of directors, to ensuring transparency,

01:13:18.180 --> 01:13:20.460
integrity, and adherence to regulations.

01:13:20.460 --> 01:13:23.020
ESG is more than just a trend.

01:13:23.020 --> 01:13:26.820
It's a strategic approach that considers the broader impact of business activities

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:31.280
on the environment, society, and ethical practices.

01:13:31.280 --> 01:13:32.900
So the origin of ESG.

01:13:32.900 --> 01:13:38.260
Although ESG has become much more well-known with people outside the investing landscape

01:13:38.260 --> 01:13:41.360
in recent years, it's existed for so long.

01:13:41.360 --> 01:13:46.400
The origins of ESG can be traced back to the mid-20th century, when discussions about corporate

01:13:46.400 --> 01:13:48.780
social responsibility started to take shape.

01:13:48.780 --> 01:13:53.780
However, it wasn't until the 1960s and 70s, fueled by social movements advocating for

01:13:53.780 --> 01:13:57.700
civil rights, that awareness of corporate impacts began to grow.

01:13:57.700 --> 01:14:02.320
This era saw the emergence of ethical investing, where investors excluded industries that conflicted

01:14:02.320 --> 01:14:04.620
with their own moral values.

01:14:04.620 --> 01:14:09.340
Global crises, including environmental disasters and financial meltdowns, further highlighted

01:14:09.340 --> 01:14:12.540
the urgent need for responsible business practices.

01:14:12.540 --> 01:14:17.420
In 2006, the United Nations took a significant step by launching the Principles for Responsible

01:14:17.420 --> 01:14:22.700
Investment, or PRI for short, signaling a formal commitment to integrating ESG considerations

01:14:22.700 --> 01:14:24.960
into investment decisions today.

01:14:24.960 --> 01:14:28.780
These historical milestones collectively paved the way for development and widespread adoption

01:14:28.780 --> 01:14:33.020
of ESG framework that we recognize today.

01:14:33.020 --> 01:14:36.980
Okay, so the importance of ESG.

01:14:36.980 --> 01:14:41.580
The significance of ESG lies in its ability to guide businesses towards long-term sustainability

01:14:41.580 --> 01:14:43.080
and success.

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:47.100
By integrating environmental, social, and governance factors, companies not only reduce

01:14:47.100 --> 01:14:54.500
their environmental footprint, companies not only reduce their environmental footprint,

01:14:54.500 --> 01:14:57.860
but also foster innovation and adapt to changing landscapes.

01:14:57.860 --> 01:15:03.020
ESG practices aren't just about doing good, they're about managing risks effectively.

01:15:03.020 --> 01:15:06.380
Identifying potential environmental and social vulnerabilities can shield a company from

01:15:06.380 --> 01:15:07.540
future disruptions.

01:15:07.540 --> 01:15:12.740
Moreover, a robust ESG strategy enhances a company's reputation, building credibility

01:15:12.740 --> 01:15:14.720
and trust with stakeholders.

01:15:14.720 --> 01:15:18.840
This is especially crucial in an era where consumers and investors expect more than just

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:20.540
financial returns.

01:15:20.540 --> 01:15:26.780
Beyond reputation, ESG practices ensure regulatory compliance as standards evolve, demonstrating

01:15:26.780 --> 01:15:29.260
a commitment to ethical conduct and transparency.

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:32.540
In essence, ESG is more than just a buzzword.

01:15:32.540 --> 01:15:37.460
It's a strategic imperative that not only ensures businesses thrive, but also contribute

01:15:37.460 --> 01:15:40.380
positively to the world around them.

01:15:40.380 --> 01:15:43.500
So let's look at it in the modern investing lens.

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:48.340
ESG has transformed the investment landscape by introducing a responsible approach to decision

01:15:48.340 --> 01:15:49.600
making.

01:15:49.600 --> 01:15:53.660
Investors now factor ESG considerations when crafting their portfolios and align their

01:15:53.660 --> 01:15:56.860
investments with ethical and sustainable values.

01:15:56.860 --> 01:16:01.260
It's not just about these values though, it's about potential financial benefits.

01:16:01.260 --> 01:16:06.420
ESG integration offers the promise of improved risk-adjusted returns, as companies with strong

01:16:06.420 --> 01:16:10.660
ESG practices often demonstrate resilience when faced with uncertainty.

01:16:10.660 --> 01:16:15.380
ESG-aligned companies also appeal to investors with a long-term outlook, as they prioritize

01:16:15.380 --> 01:16:21.540
sustainability in both environmental and self-sustainability and are better equipped to weather market

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:22.540
fluctuations.

01:16:22.540 --> 01:16:30.340
Notably, research indicates that companies excelling in ESG can even outperform their

01:16:30.340 --> 01:16:31.940
peers financially.

01:16:31.940 --> 01:16:38.060
Beyond profit, ESG opens doors to impact investing, where investors actively contribute to positive

01:16:38.060 --> 01:16:41.940
societal and environmental changes through their investment choices.

01:16:41.940 --> 01:16:47.420
The integration of ESG factors signal a fundamental shift in how investors perceive value, emphasizing

01:16:47.420 --> 01:16:53.420
both financial returns and contributions to a more sustainable world.

01:16:53.420 --> 01:16:56.020
This is especially important in a modern world.

01:16:56.020 --> 01:17:00.420
With factors like social media, ESG has become even more important, and companies that fail

01:17:00.420 --> 01:17:05.300
to adopt ESG are placed under much more scrutiny as we'll see in future examples, compared

01:17:05.300 --> 01:17:09.140
to companies who do adopt ESG.

01:17:09.140 --> 01:17:12.940
The impact on stakeholders ESG doesn't just affect a company's internal

01:17:12.940 --> 01:17:16.100
practices, it extends to its relationship with stakeholders.

01:17:16.100 --> 01:17:19.860
Employees, for instance, are more engaged and satisfied when they work for a company

01:17:19.860 --> 01:17:25.900
that prioritizes their well-being and supports a positive workplace culture through ESG practices.

01:17:25.900 --> 01:17:31.220
Customers too are drawn to brands that align with their own values, and ESG provides that

01:17:31.220 --> 01:17:32.500
connection.

01:17:32.500 --> 01:17:36.900
Companies with strong ESG values tend to attract and retain socially conscious customers, thus

01:17:36.900 --> 01:17:39.220
strengthening brand loyalty.

01:17:39.220 --> 01:17:44.020
Beyond employees and customers, ESG initiatives have a positive ripple effect on their local

01:17:44.020 --> 01:17:49.260
communities, contributing to societal well-being and forging deeper community connections.

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:53.780
Investors and other stakeholders also expect transparency and ethical conduct, making ESG

01:17:53.780 --> 01:17:56.260
an essential aspect of building trust.

01:17:56.260 --> 01:18:01.700
Ultimately, effective ESG practices manage not only a company's reputation, but also

01:18:01.700 --> 01:18:07.340
its relationship with the broader ecosystem that it operates within.

01:18:07.340 --> 01:18:09.820
The global impact of ESG

01:18:09.820 --> 01:18:14.420
As we said before, ESG is not limited to companies and not just communities either, and it has

01:18:14.420 --> 01:18:16.900
a significant effect on global outreach.

01:18:16.900 --> 01:18:21.700
By prioritizing environmental sustainability, ESG practices align with global objectives,

01:18:21.700 --> 01:18:25.340
such as mitigating climate change and conserving biodiversity.

01:18:25.340 --> 01:18:30.540
On the social front, ESG initiatives address pressing challenges like poverty and inequality,

01:18:30.540 --> 01:18:33.140
contributing to a broader global development.

01:18:33.140 --> 01:18:38.140
The collaborative nature of ESG becomes evident through partnerships between companies, governments,

01:18:38.140 --> 01:18:42.980
and organizations across borders, all working together to address shared challenges.

01:18:42.980 --> 01:18:48.020
ESG encourages companies to innovate sustainably, develop solutions that can drive positive

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:52.540
impacts on a global scale, and innovate more.

01:18:52.540 --> 01:18:58.420
So, as a financial investor, how do you assess a business's ESG?

01:18:58.420 --> 01:19:02.900
Now that we've established that ESG is important, how do we go about measuring it?

01:19:02.900 --> 01:19:07.780
Assessing a company's ESG performance involves evaluating its practices across environmental,

01:19:07.780 --> 01:19:09.860
social, and governance dimensions.

01:19:09.860 --> 01:19:15.500
ESG rating agencies play a pivotal role, providing scores based on established criteria that

01:19:15.500 --> 01:19:19.460
measure companies' alignment with ESG practices.

01:19:19.460 --> 01:19:24.340
In some sectors, specific ESG standards are in place, setting expectations for how companies

01:19:24.340 --> 01:19:27.100
should operate sustainably.

01:19:27.100 --> 01:19:31.140
As you can see on screen, there is something called the MSCI ESG rating, which is one of

01:19:31.140 --> 01:19:33.660
the ESG rating agencies.

01:19:33.660 --> 01:19:39.260
Additionally, transparency is key, as companies share their ESG data through reports disclosing

01:19:39.260 --> 01:19:41.300
their efforts and impacts.

01:19:41.300 --> 01:19:46.500
A critical aspect is materiality, which means focusing on the ESG factors most relevant

01:19:46.500 --> 01:19:48.460
to a company and its industry.

01:19:48.460 --> 01:19:51.380
This ensures that efforts are targeted where they matter most.

01:19:51.380 --> 01:19:55.860
ESG assessments serve as a dynamic tool for companies not just to measure their current

01:19:55.860 --> 01:20:00.740
performance, but to identify areas for growth and enhancement.

01:20:00.740 --> 01:20:05.340
So, how can businesses incorporate ESG?

01:20:05.340 --> 01:20:10.420
Incorporating ESG into business operations requires a nuanced and comprehensive approach.

01:20:10.420 --> 01:20:15.260
It starts with strong leadership commitment, with top management actively championing ESG

01:20:15.260 --> 01:20:16.260
principles.

01:20:16.260 --> 01:20:20.620
It can't just be an afterthought, it needs to be integrated into the core business strategy,

01:20:20.620 --> 01:20:26.020
along with the business principles and the business plan, which we discussed earlier.

01:20:26.020 --> 01:20:27.700
Stakeholder engagement is critical.

01:20:27.700 --> 01:20:31.860
Making employees, customers, and partners ensure that ESG initiatives align with their

01:20:31.860 --> 01:20:33.460
needs and concerns.

01:20:33.460 --> 01:20:39.220
To measure progress, establish clear ESG metrics and targets, or maybe consult ESG agencies.

01:20:39.220 --> 01:20:42.100
This helps hold companies accountable for their efforts.

01:20:42.100 --> 01:20:46.620
Additionally, awareness is key, so employees understand the significance of ESG and their

01:20:46.620 --> 01:20:49.220
role and its implementation.

01:20:49.220 --> 01:20:53.260
Innovation thrives when ESG goals are aligned, so companies should encourage research and

01:20:53.260 --> 01:20:57.900
development that supports sustainability.

01:20:57.900 --> 01:21:00.340
So ESG vs non-ESG stocks.

01:21:00.340 --> 01:21:04.580
Although we've clearly established that ESG does have benefits, how does it really help

01:21:04.580 --> 01:21:05.580
in reality?

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:07.860
Theory isn't always right.

01:21:07.860 --> 01:21:09.260
So let's look at in reality.

01:21:09.260 --> 01:21:13.940
The choice between ESG and non-ESG stocks has had significant implications for investors.

01:21:13.940 --> 01:21:18.660
ESG-aligned companies tend to demonstrate better risk management and resilience against

01:21:18.660 --> 01:21:21.460
market turbulence, as we mentioned earlier.

01:21:21.460 --> 01:21:25.700
Research indicates that over the long term, ESG stocks can potentially outperform their

01:21:25.700 --> 01:21:30.540
non-ESG counterparts, driven by sustainable practices and responsible strategies.

01:21:30.540 --> 01:21:35.260
Moreover, as the demand for ethical and sustainable investments grow, especially in the modern

01:21:35.260 --> 01:21:39.540
world, ESG stocks could experience increased investor interest.

01:21:39.540 --> 01:21:45.100
ESG-aligned companies may also face fewer regulatory hurdles and reputational risks,

01:21:45.100 --> 01:21:47.460
making them more potentially stable choices.

01:21:47.460 --> 01:21:52.660
For example, they may not be affected by laws governing carbon taxes, and they may not have

01:21:52.660 --> 01:21:57.580
fit quotas which most companies that are non-ESG focused may fail to meet.

01:21:57.580 --> 01:22:01.820
Beyond just financial gains, investing in ESG stocks also aligns with broader trends

01:22:01.820 --> 01:22:06.480
in responsible investing and sustainable consumer behavior.

01:22:06.480 --> 01:22:11.100
So let's look at a mock situation and compare two companies, one which follows ESG practices

01:22:11.100 --> 01:22:13.100
and one that doesn't.

01:22:13.100 --> 01:22:19.340
The company that produces renewable energy has a strong track record of reducing carbon

01:22:19.340 --> 01:22:21.860
emissions and promoting clear energy.

01:22:21.860 --> 01:22:25.980
As a result, they have experienced steady growth driven by an increased demand for renewable

01:22:25.980 --> 01:22:32.700
energy sources and favorable regulatory policies which may reduce tax or provide subsidies.

01:22:32.700 --> 01:22:37.500
Additionally, they'll experience positive media, which can also help boost the company.

01:22:37.500 --> 01:22:41.120
On the other hand, a company which relies on fossil fuels will experience criticism

01:22:41.120 --> 01:22:44.940
for their environmental impact and negative media due to their contributions to climate

01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:45.940
change.

01:22:45.940 --> 01:22:50.660
As a result, their stock is likely to be volatile due to fluctuating oil prices in a dynamic

01:22:50.660 --> 01:22:56.180
market, changing that market dynamics, and growing concerns about environmental sustainability.

01:22:56.180 --> 01:22:59.620
As a result, in this situation, it's clear that the prior case is better when they use

01:22:59.620 --> 01:23:00.620
renewable energy.

01:23:00.620 --> 01:23:05.980
Additionally, let's look at a second example, but this time focusing on the social and governance

01:23:05.980 --> 01:23:07.380
aspects.

01:23:07.380 --> 01:23:12.940
In a diverse workplace, a company that promotes a gender and ethnic diversity and a workforce

01:23:12.940 --> 01:23:17.540
that represents a wide range of backgrounds is more likely to succeed than one that doesn't

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:19.380
have these same practices.

01:23:19.380 --> 01:23:23.620
This is because employees are likely to be more motivated in the workplace and media

01:23:23.620 --> 01:23:27.940
is likely to show companies that focus on diversity in a positive light, whereas companies

01:23:27.940 --> 01:23:31.060
that don't focus are likely to face criticism.

01:23:31.060 --> 01:23:34.560
However, this is a nuanced approach.

01:23:34.560 --> 01:23:39.100
We need to look at reality, and in some cases, companies that don't have ESG principles

01:23:39.100 --> 01:23:41.900
in the moment may outperform those which do.

01:23:41.900 --> 01:23:46.440
However, in the long run, it's important to note that as the world becomes increasingly

01:23:46.440 --> 01:23:52.540
focused on social objectives, ESG will grow in importance, and as a result, non-ESG companies

01:23:52.540 --> 01:23:59.260
that may be in a better position now may not be in that same position in the future.

01:23:59.260 --> 01:24:04.340
So in this section, we'll just go over how to construct a portfolio and diversification,

01:24:04.340 --> 01:24:08.720
and the types of management, and the basics of how you construct a portfolio.

01:24:08.720 --> 01:24:12.180
So first of all, what is diversification?

01:24:12.180 --> 01:24:18.100
Diversification is essentially the process of purchasing assets from different asset

01:24:18.100 --> 01:24:23.540
classes to expand the number and type of securities in your portfolio.

01:24:23.540 --> 01:24:28.540
So if we look at this diagram, we can see here that on the x-axis, there's a number

01:24:28.540 --> 01:24:33.380
of securities, and on the y-axis, there's a standard deviation of your portfolio's

