01:25:16.780 --> 01:25:20.100
Okay, so what are the types of risk?

01:25:20.100 --> 01:25:23.620
Well, there's systematic and unsystematic risk.

01:25:23.620 --> 01:25:28.460
Systematic risk is a market risk, which is non-diversable, which means you can get rid

01:25:28.460 --> 01:25:33.500
of it if you diversify, and it stems from market-wide risks.

01:25:33.500 --> 01:25:37.140
Unsystematic risks, on the other hand, are company or industry-specific risks that are

01:25:37.140 --> 01:25:40.180
inherent to a specific investment.

01:25:40.180 --> 01:25:46.300
Systematic risks are non-diversifiable, which means that they cannot get rid of.

01:25:46.300 --> 01:25:51.220
This is a risk that remains after efforts have been taken to diversify a portfolio.

01:25:51.220 --> 01:25:56.220
Systematic risk incorporates things like interest rate changes, inflation, recessions, and wars,

01:25:56.220 --> 01:25:58.660
among other major changes.

01:25:58.660 --> 01:26:02.620
Shifts in these things can affect the entire market and cannot be mitigated by changing

01:26:02.620 --> 01:26:07.500
positions within a portfolio of equities, because it affects every single equity.

01:26:07.500 --> 01:26:12.100
For example, the global financial crisis is a great example of systematic risk.

01:26:12.100 --> 01:26:16.300
Anyone who invested in the market in 2008 saw the values of their investment change

01:26:16.300 --> 01:26:18.740
drastically from this economic event.

01:26:18.740 --> 01:26:23.220
The Great Recession affected asset classes in different ways, as riskier securities,

01:26:23.220 --> 01:26:27.220
typically those that were leveraged more, were sold off in large quantities, whereas

01:26:27.220 --> 01:26:31.620
simpler assets like treasury bonds became more valuable.

01:26:31.620 --> 01:26:37.020
Unsystematic risk is a company or industry-specific risk that is inherent with each investment,

01:26:37.020 --> 01:26:44.380
and other systematic risks can be reduced by diversifying, as we've seen in this diagram.

01:26:44.380 --> 01:26:48.900
Systematic risks include things like inflation risk, interest rate risk, exchange rate risk,

01:26:48.900 --> 01:26:51.620
and things like pandemics and war.

01:26:51.620 --> 01:26:55.980
Net risks are things like business risk, financial risk, default risk, and liquidity risk, which

01:26:55.980 --> 01:26:59.700
are specific to certain companies.

01:26:59.700 --> 01:27:02.780
So how do you measure your portfolio's performance?

01:27:02.780 --> 01:27:07.980
Well, the best way to do so is using benchmarks.

01:27:07.980 --> 01:27:13.020
A benchmark is a standard tool or measure that you can use to analyze the risk of a

01:27:13.020 --> 01:27:15.060
gear and portfolio.

01:27:15.060 --> 01:27:20.540
Individual funds and investment portfolios will typically have an established benchmark

01:27:20.540 --> 01:27:27.740
that you can already use for standard analysis to check your level of risk.

01:27:27.740 --> 01:27:30.820
Time-weighted vs. dollar-weighted returns

01:27:30.820 --> 01:27:34.900
Time-weighted returns are determined without regard to any subsequent cash flows from the

01:27:34.900 --> 01:27:35.900
investor.

01:27:35.900 --> 01:27:39.780
It measures the performance of the investment over a certain period of time, not the returns

01:27:39.780 --> 01:27:41.620
of the investor.

01:27:41.620 --> 01:27:45.900
Returns quoted by mutual funds and other investment managers are time-weighted because the portfolio

01:27:45.900 --> 01:27:50.300
manager doesn't have any control over future cash flows of investor dollars.

01:27:50.300 --> 01:27:55.260
In contrast, a dollar-weighted return considers subsequent contributions to and withdraws

01:27:55.260 --> 01:27:56.860
from an investment.

01:27:56.860 --> 01:28:00.780
For example, it considers timings and sales of purchases.

01:28:00.780 --> 01:28:05.740
A dollar-weighted approach focuses on the returns of the investor over a period of time,

01:28:05.740 --> 01:28:10.700
and this will usually differ from the time-weighted return.

01:28:10.700 --> 01:28:13.580
So how do you measure portfolio risk?

01:28:13.580 --> 01:28:17.300
Well, to measure risk, we need to identify what risk is.

01:28:17.300 --> 01:28:24.140
As we mentioned earlier, risk is the chance that a stock might fail, or the risk of volatility.

01:28:24.140 --> 01:28:28.060
So to measure risk, we would measure the chance that the performance of your portfolio as

01:28:28.060 --> 01:28:33.960
a whole would differ from the returns of the market or the benchmark portfolio.

01:28:33.960 --> 01:28:38.560
And the best way to calculate risk is using a standard deviation, which gives you returns

01:28:38.560 --> 01:28:40.440
over a given period.

01:28:40.440 --> 01:28:45.060
Standard deviation is the measure of dispersion of a set of data from its mean.

01:28:45.060 --> 01:28:49.460
It measures the absolute variability of a distribution.

01:28:49.460 --> 01:28:57.420
In essence, the greater the standard deviation, the greater the deviation from the mean.

01:28:57.420 --> 01:28:59.860
And this is the formula to calculate standard deviation.

01:28:59.860 --> 01:29:03.320
If any of you have taken statistics before, you'd be able to understand it.

01:29:03.320 --> 01:29:07.620
But if not, it's not necessary that you understand how to calculate standard deviation to actually

01:29:07.620 --> 01:29:08.660
employ it.

01:29:08.660 --> 01:29:15.340
You can use any online calculator to actually figure it out.

01:29:15.340 --> 01:29:17.980
So here is a standard deviation belt curve.

01:29:17.980 --> 01:29:22.560
Essentially the areas within the curve are more or the higher likely opportunities.

01:29:22.560 --> 01:29:29.620
So in this general area, 68.27% of opportunities will follow this.

