However, the rate at which it does so is diminishing.

01:29:40.540 --> 01:29:43.180
So active versus passive management.

01:29:43.180 --> 01:29:48.020
Passive investment strategies seek to replicate the constituents, weights, and therefore the

01:29:48.020 --> 01:29:51.820
risk and returns of a specific benchmark of index securities.

01:29:51.820 --> 01:29:56.220
It's a by-and-hold strategy that does not seek to pick winners and losers among the

01:29:56.220 --> 01:29:59.820
securities, but rather just hold them and see how things play out.

01:29:59.820 --> 01:30:04.380
The benefits of passive management include low fees, enhanced tax efficiency that stems

01:30:04.380 --> 01:30:09.560
from low portfolio turnover, and a limited portfolio management infrastructure, essentially

01:30:09.560 --> 01:30:11.640
meaning it's easier to manage yourself.

01:30:11.640 --> 01:30:17.220
The most popular use of vehicles for passive investment include index mutual funds and

01:30:17.220 --> 01:30:20.580
ETFs, or exchange traded funds.

01:30:20.580 --> 01:30:24.820
Active management, on the other hand, is based on the belief that risk-adjusted returns above

01:30:24.820 --> 01:30:29.860
the benchmark can be achieved through security selection, market timing, and sector rotations

01:30:29.860 --> 01:30:33.020
by highly skilled professional portfolio managers.

01:30:33.020 --> 01:30:37.580
Actually meaning you pick and choose stocks based on how you think they're going to do.

01:30:37.580 --> 01:30:42.060
Actively managed portfolios generally result in significant portfolio turnover as the manager

01:30:42.060 --> 01:30:47.900
reacts to market and temporary trends, either observed or forecasted.

01:30:47.900 --> 01:30:52.140
This high turnover combined with the costs to staff our portfolio and management team

01:30:52.140 --> 01:30:56.440
requires the manager to charge significantly higher fees than they would charge for a passively

01:30:56.440 --> 01:31:01.860
managed portfolio, and generally results in poor tax efficiency as frequent buying and

01:31:01.860 --> 01:31:07.780
selling of securities may result in something called a capital gains tax, which is essentially

01:31:07.780 --> 01:31:10.700
the tax you pay when you sell stocks.

01:31:10.700 --> 01:31:14.660
This capital gains tax is greater when you sell a stock within a period of less than

01:31:14.660 --> 01:31:19.220
a year, and it's less when you sell a stock that you've held for greater than a year.

01:31:19.220 --> 01:31:26.540
In the past, the S&amp;P 500 has had significantly more success than actively traded portfolios.

01:31:26.540 --> 01:31:30.980
This has been occurring for the past 20 years, and you could replicate the index yourself.

01:31:30.980 --> 01:31:35.580
For example, you could buy all 500 stocks in the S&amp;P 500 in the same proportion that

01:31:35.580 --> 01:31:38.500
they are in the S&amp;P 500 index.

